{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls scripts; cat figure_manifest.json; cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat paper_evidence_audit_feedback.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; sed -n '1,110p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '1,260p' scripts/build_figures.py; cat figure_manifest.json.prov.json; ls -l /tmp | head; command -v apply_patch; rg --files /opt /tmp /usr/local/bin 2>/dev/null | rg '(apply_patch|pdftotext|pdfinfo|validate_figures)' | head -25\"","aggregated_output":"__pycache__\nacquire_scores.py\nassemble_references.py\naudit_paper.py\nbuild_claims.py\nbuild_provenance.py\nbuild_results.py\nfetch_exact_references.py\nlog_event.py\nreproduce_allocations.py\nstage_resources.py\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"attempted_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        5\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9,\n      \"fallback_reason\": \"generate-concept-figure exited non-zero (rc=1)\",\n      \"figure_id\": \"score_space_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 11\n      },\n      \"generator\": null,\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/score_space_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/score_space_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        4,\n        4,\n        4,\n        4\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"figure_id\": \"paired_mean_median_budgets\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 11\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 4,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/paired_mean_median_budgets.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/paired_mean_median_budgets.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        4,\n        4,\n        4,\n        4\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"figure_id\": \"cap_conditioned_reachability\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 11\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/cap_conditioned_reachability.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/cap_conditioned_reachability.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-07T05:06:57.986334+00:00`\n- ok: `True`\n- verdict: `pass`\n- finding_count: `0`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/a01/codex/20261107_023211/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/a01/codex/20261107_023211/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/a01/codex/20261107_023211/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nMain and supplementary claims match the grounding and evidence trail, with search results, final qualification, and scientific limitations kept distinct.\n\n## Findings\n- None.\n{\n  \"status\": \"no_gaps\",\n  \"summary\": \"The draft directly cites its relevant comparator literatures and benchmark background, while its allocation claims are grounded in recorded evidence or explicit derivations, so no targeted post-draft retrieval is justified.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n# Post-draft citation-gap analysis\n\n**Status:** `no_gaps`\n\nThe draft directly cites its relevant comparator literatures and benchmark background, while its allocation claims are grounded in recorded evidence or explicit derivations, so no targeted post-draft retrieval is justified.\n\n## Coverage supporting this decision\n\n- **Introduction** (`paper.tex:20`): MMLU, BIG-Bench Hard, and HELM have direct citations. The median-resistance intuition at `paper.tex:24` is explicitly qualified, not asserted as an established leaderboard guarantee; robust-estimation background is cited in Related Work.\n- **Related Work — Evaluation design and incentives** (`paper.tex:39`): benchmark selection, evaluation infrastructure, leaderboard incentives, and strategic post-training each have direct citations. The paragraph distinguishes the present fixed-rival allocation model from strategic equilibrium analysis.\n- **Related Work — Social choice and targeted changes** (`paper.tex:42`): social-choice evaluation, rank aggregation, strategic reporting, and election bribery are directly cited. The target-only, capped intervention is distinguished from those models rather than presented as an uncited external comparator family.\n- **Related Work — Robustness and contamination** (`paper.tex:45`): robust estimation, influence, and contamination have direct citations. The paragraph disclaims any empirical calibration of the hypothetical headroom response from those studies.\n- **Method** (`paper.tex:73`): aggregation rules and comparator policies are explicitly defined; allocation certificates are derived and linked to implementation evidence. These are not unsupported claims of a generic greedy guarantee or optimizer superiority. The supplementary discussion at `paper.tex:260` already cites submodular optimization and robust optimization when explaining their different assumptions.\n- **Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol** (`paper.tex:131`): snapshot dimensions, normalization, target selection, and allocation checks are grounded in frozen resources and evaluation records. The existing **Public snapshots, normalization, and reproduction map** subsection (`paper.tex:388`) supplies acquisition and version context; the draft does not equate these recomputed comparisons with live leaderboard rankings.\n\n## Grounding and exclusions\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, `references_provenance.json`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. All 25 distinct citation keys used in the manuscript resolve in the bibliography and have provenance entries with existing local consulted-content artifacts. This establishes current coverage, not an exhaustive literature review or a new full-text verification of every reference.\n\nThe strategy's earlier warnings about incomplete reference verification describe a pre-draft state; current bibliography, provenance, and claim mappings document subsequent consultation. The global memory summary is qualitative context only. Recorded quantitative findings, original mathematical arguments, and explicitly acknowledged training-response limitations are not retrieval gaps. Seeking additional manipulation variants or training-cost studies would expand positioning rather than repair a visibly unsupported paragraph.\n\n**Missing literatures:** none. **Rewrite targets:** none. **Failure reason:** none.\n\nNo references were retrieved. The manuscript, bibliography, and reference-provenance file were not modified.\n# Paper strategy: cap-dependent task budgets\n\n**Status:** ready for the `paper_standalone` family. This is a framing plan, not a manuscript or a statement that final submission requirements have already been met.\n\n**Provisional title:** Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion.\n\n## Central question and thesis\n\n**Question.** On fixed public multitask score tables, how does the smallest task subset needed to make a target co-first change with the aggregation rule and the allowed fraction of score headroom?\n\n**Thesis.** In the evaluated score tables, changing the response cap reverses mean-versus-median investment orderings for some targets and makes other promotions unattainable. Aggregation-rule resistance must therefore be reported conditional on the cap and target score vector, rather than as a universal ranking.\n\nPresent HELM MMLU and the six-task Open LLM table as two settings of one static intervention model. Do not organize them as chronological experiments. The contribution is a conditional, reproducible score-space analysis with explicit allocation certificates and failure cases, not a training method, an architecture, or a demonstrated optimizer improvement.\n\n## Framing decision and alternatives\n\nThis is not an edit pass. No seeded prior manuscript, resolved edit-context files, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` were found. The JSON enum `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` means no inherited manuscript story is adopted; it does not mean an unseen manuscript was assessed.\n\nThe selected source and original comparator were inspected directly. The selected staged `program.py` matches the downstream source file by SHA-256. Selection metadata identifies the first candidate, not either later refinement. Recorded directory-tree hashes differ across artifact scopes; do not equate them based on a matching source file.\n\nThree candidate stories were considered:\n\n- **Prospective cap frontiers:** preserve the earlier conceptual direction, but narrow its language. Only two positive caps were evaluated; continuous frontiers, empirical response envelopes, strict-margin sensitivity, and composition resampling are not completed evidence.\n- **Broad cross-setting solver improvements:** reject as the center. There are no grounded ablations attributing gains to the later gain-window or mixture-cut variants, and search scores tie the original comparator.\n- **Measured cap-dependent promotion budgets:** select. The same-target rule comparisons directly demonstrate reversals in both score tables, while reachability and independent bounds make the limitations inspectable.\n\nThis choice beats a campaign diary because it answers the original scientific question with controlled, interpretable comparisons. Neither a positive search winner nor model training is needed for this bounded study.\n\n## Independent final assessment and evidence hierarchy\n\nRead final assessment independently of selection. The following are **private audit facts**, not names for visible scientific metrics:\n\n- `study.selection_kind = non_winner_candidate`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`.\n- `study.search_score = study.baseline_score = 0.7239766081871346`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`.\n- `study.attempts.total/scored/unscored = 3/3/0`. Unscored attempts would be missing performance evidence, not measured losses.\n- `final.qualification.status/outcome/correct = completed/qualified/true`.\n- `final.metric.final_score = 0.6633040935672518` belongs to a separate weighted evaluation scope. It is not a matched change from the search score.\n\nNo failed final qualification is reported in this run. Do not invent one, reinterpret non-selection as a validity failure, or treat passing assessment as proof of generalization or scientific novelty. Missing criterion diagnostics remain unknown. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; use recorded independent validation and reference bounds instead of self-report.\n\nMain quantitative grounding is `final.evidence.episodes`, whose payload is `paper_data.json.final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes`. Filter `solution.cases` to `case = original`, then pair by family, target, rule, fraction, and seed. Retain exact episode/case/observation pointers for downstream claim mapping. The method files and `task/resources/final/` files support implementation and provenance explanations; their paths are not invented grounding IDs.\n\n## Scientific definitions and scope\n\nFor normalized target scores `x` and a selected task subset `S`, selecting coordinate `j` changes it to `round(x_j + rho(1-x_j), 10)`. Other target coordinates and every rival score coordinate remain fixed. Investment is the uniform task count `|S|`, optionally divided by task count `d` for cross-setting display. The empirical positive caps are `rho = 1` and `rho = 0.5`; zero belongs to the no-update control.\n\nPromotion means **co-first** within aggregate tolerance `1e-10`, not strict first place. The ordinary even median averages its two central values. Borda uses average pairwise win credit with half credit for ties: changing the target can change rivals' Borda totals despite fixed rival task scores. Recompute those totals.\n\nThis is a hypothetical score-response model. It does not measure training examples, tokens, money, compute, contamination efficacy, transfer, forgetting, or rivals' strategic responses. Only under a genuine upper-response envelope could an exact support minimum lower-bound affected tasks for a successful intervention; no evidence here calibrates such an envelope to real training.\n\nThe frozen final context contains 57 HELM subjects and six normalized Open LLM columns, with 42 and 2129 rows respectively. These are resource-context facts, not the broader public-catalog totals from the task brief. Do not mix Raw columns into the six-task analysis or reinterpret their aggregate columns as all underlying subtasks. Target row numbers are local identifiers, not model names.\n\n## Narrow method groups\n\n1. **Static capped-response model, supporting:** the shared intervention and the three aggregation rules define the comparison.\n2. **Frozen allocation solver, studied/proposed:** sorted gains for mean, threshold counting for odd median, endpoint counting for even median, and bounded rival-aware integer Borda search with verified fallback. The label proposed does not assert novelty or superiority.\n3. **Original frozen comparator, baseline:** preserve its tied search comparison using `study.baseline_score` and `study.search_score_delta`. No separate matched final evaluation of that full implementation is grounded.\n4. **Evaluator-recorded policies, baselines:** no update, random task order, and immediate-gain greedy. These have matched final allocation records; do not silently relabel the evaluator's greedy policy as the original frozen comparator.\n5. **Independent budget reference, supporting:** exact flags and lower/upper bounds certify individual reported costs. Finite selected allocations match exact reference cardinalities in the original records. This is not a proof that the deadline-bounded Borda algorithm is exact on every input.\n\nExclude later gain-window and mixture-cut branches from the method narrative. They are not ablations, and their names are not evidence of an improved mechanism. Do not introduce unmeasured architecture shifts or training procedures.\n\n## Results that carry the argument\n\n### Cap-conditioned budget ordering\n\nPut this small table in the main body; each entry is a task count from `final.evidence.episodes`, not a training cost:\n\n| Score table | Local target row | Headroom fraction | Mean | Median | Borda |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| HELM | 10 | 1 | 9 | 12 | 34 |\n| HELM | 10 | 0.5 | 23 | 12 | 39 |\n| Open LLM | 1609 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 5 |\n| Open LLM | 1609 | 0.5 | 4 | 3 | 5 |\n\nThe within-target mean/median ordering reverses in each setting. These are counterexamples to an unconditional ordering, not estimates of reversal frequency in future model populations. Explain them using cumulative score gains versus order-statistic thresholds, not an untested causal account of developer behavior.\n\nShow all sampled targets in the main comparison, not only these examples. Targets were selected as nonwinners under the original **mean**, not under every rule. HELM row 40 already needs zero median updates; retain that zero without attributing it to an intervention.\n\n### Reachability, not fictitious finite budgets\n\nAt full headroom, all eight sampled targets per family are reachable under each rule. At half headroom, mean/median/Borda reachability counts are **5/5/4 for HELM** and **7/6/3 for Open LLM**, derived from the original final records. Keep the denominator at eight targets per family, not seeds times targets or controls plus originals.\n\nReport unreachable promotion as a status or infinity. Empty task lists accompanying unattainability are not zero-cost successes. Reference sentinels `d+1` are not investment values. Do not average away unattainable cases or compare finite-only averages without stating the changed population.\n\n### Allocation context and controls\n\nAll original reference records in each family report exact bounds; feasible selected allocations match them. Explain the difference between an exact measured instance and a universally exact algorithm. A matched comparator example can show why immediate-gain greedy is not a median certificate: HELM row 28 at half headroom has median cost 21 versus the recorded greedy cost 52. Do not use this isolated comparison to claim general solver superiority.\n\nSelected original allocations repeat across the three recorded seeds. These are repeated static instances, not independently trained models or random population samples. Report repeatability and exact integers; do not invent confidence intervals, significance tests, or population uncertainty. Random-order baseline summaries may use only the recorded orders/seeds with their limited coverage disclosed.\n\nPermutation, constant-score, and zero-update cases are implementation controls. Keep them separate from empirical budget and reachability denominators. A synthetic constant table is not another public benchmark. Do not claim a completed replay result when its retained value is null.\n\n## Required figures and physical layout\n\n`paper.sty` sets a **single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height**. Full text width and column width are therefore the same here; 0.48 text width is not a conference column. All three figures target width fraction 1.0. The generic historical style header does not authenticate the official ICLR 2026 template: verify official venue files before final drafting/submission.\n\n### Figure 1: score-space overview\n\n- **Generator:** `generate-concept-figure`, later orchestrator-owned; do not run it during strategy generation.\n- **Content:** public score table and frozen target/rivals, capped target-coordinate updates, the three rules, and minimum task count or unattainability. State that no training is performed. Use an equation and labels, not invented scores.\n- **Placement:** page 1 or 2, within the introduction/problem transition.\n- **Footprint:** approximately 5.5 by 2.3 inches plus caption, about 0.30 body page.\n- **Readability budget:** one panel, at most five major blocks and three rule branches; labels at least 9 pt. The arrows describe the mathematical mechanism, not research chronology.\n\n### Figure 2: paired mean/median budgets\n\n- **Generator:** `matplotlib`; four panels arranged 2 by 2, dataset by response cap.\n- **Axes:** exact/bounded minimum mean task fraction on x and median task fraction on y, both on `[0,1]`, with an equality diagonal.\n- **Data:** all eight original targets per panel; use no more than four status encodings. Eight points are observations, not eight colored series. Put unreachable cases in separate labeled marginal rails, with a corner category for both unreachable; never place a fabricated finite value on either numeric axis.\nsed: can't read scripts/build_figures.py: No such file or directory\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\n    \"path\": \"figure_manifest.json\",\n    \"kind\": \"derived\"\n  },\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[paper_strategy.json]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": [\n    \"Parent orchestration renders the overview and recompiles the PDF after this stage. Mutable output has an input trail but no pre-render content digest.\"\n  ]\n}total 0\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 root root 60 Oct 26 13:58 node-compile-cache\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 root root 40 Oct 26 13:58 opencode\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/tool_choice_apply_patch.py\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.1